{
  "result": "Here is a 250-word free write:\n\nI didn't expect to find peace in a laundromat at two in the morning, but there I was. The dryers hummed a low, warm chorus, and the fluorescent lights buzzed overhead. Outside, the city was quiet in a way it never is during the day, rain tapping the windows like a shy visitor. \nI was waiting for my clothes to spin, scrolling mindlessly, when an older woman sat down beside me with a basket of neatly folded towels. She smiled and offered me a hard candy from her purse, the kind with strawberry wrappers. I took it, and we started talking. \nShe told me about her son who lived overseas, how she still washed his old t-shirts even though he hadn't been home in years. She didn't say it with sadness, just with a gentle matter-of-factness, as if love is simply something you keep doing, even when no one's there to see it. \nThat struck me. How much of what we do is quiet maintenance for things we hope will return? We water plants, we keep photos, we re-read messages. We tend to the empty rooms. \nMy dryer beeped. I folded my clothes more slowly than usual, thinking about all the small rituals that hold us together when nothing big seems to happen. Maybe peace isn't a grand revelation. Maybe it's just a warm laundromat, a strawberry candy, and a stranger reminding you to keep folding. And somehow, that was enough to make the night feel less lonely. Truly enough.",
